00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:40.253
- Hey, LegalEagles.

00:00:40.253 --> 00:00:42.460
It's time to think like
a District Attorney.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.330
Now, what are the
responsibilities of grand jurors?

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.100
How much influence does
the prosecutor have?

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.220
And what is the situation
for Trump and his employees?

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:51.750
Today, we're gonna learn the ins and outs

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.676
of the grand jury process in New York.

00:00:53.676 --> 00:00:55.137
But the first question is,

00:00:55.137 --> 00:00:58.580
"How easy is it to indict
someone with a grand jury?"

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.920
Well, former New York State
Chief Judge Sol Wachtler

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.750
once said the prosecutors
had so much control

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.358
over the grand jury that by and large,

00:01:04.358 --> 00:01:07.489
they could get them to
indict a ham sandwich.

00:01:07.489 --> 00:01:09.360
Now this phrase has become shorthand

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.050
for prosecutors' ability
to get grand juries

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:12.290
to do whatever they want.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:13.632
And as we'll discuss
later on in this video,

00:01:13.632 --> 00:01:15.940
that control can go both ways.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.890
And ironically, Wachtler
himself was later indicted

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.210
for hush money payments to a mistress.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:24.400
But back to the story at hand,
what is going on with Trump?

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.050
Well, the Washington Post reported that

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.210
a Manhattan grand jury had
recently started to meet

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.410
to review potential charges

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.540
against the Trump organization
and its principles.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.420
So this information is not entirely new.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:35.970
Vance's office has been investigating the

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.110
Trump organization for about two years.

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:39.940
We don't know all of the
details, but tax fraud

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:43.010
and campaign finance violations
are both on the table.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:44.157
Former Trump lawyer, Michael Cohen,

00:01:44.157 --> 00:01:45.818
has been interviewed eight times

00:01:45.818 --> 00:01:47.410
by Vance's office.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.300
And Cohen has discussed
hush money payments

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.697
he allegedly made to Trump's mistresses.

00:01:50.697 --> 00:01:52.359
In addition to providing background

00:01:52.359 --> 00:01:55.060
on how Trump's businesses were operated.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.540
Last year, I did a video about the

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.640
explosive allegations of Trump tax fraud

00:01:58.640 --> 00:01:59.910
by the New York Times.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.010
Vance's office as believed to
be looking at whether Trump

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.790
or his employees lied
about the value of assets

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.557
to gain favorable loan
terms and tax benefits.

00:02:07.557 --> 00:02:10.157
Vance's investigation got
a boost earlier this year

00:02:10.157 --> 00:02:12.030
after the Supreme Court ruled that Trump

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.560
had to turn over eight
years of tax records.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.460
Reports say that these
records include both the final

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.080
and draft version of tax returns.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.840
And this information
would be central to any

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.550
determination that Trump
himself had criminal liability

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.391
especially with regards
to his mental state

00:02:24.391 --> 00:02:26.960
when these financial documents were filed.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.410
And as we discussed in the
video about Trump's taxes,

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.420
it's not easy to prove tax fraud.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.418
Tax fraud turns on whether
the person filing the return

00:02:33.418 --> 00:02:35.600
knew that they were committing fraud.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.479
Powerful people like CEOs and Mob Bosses

00:02:37.479 --> 00:02:40.100
are usually pretty savvy
about speaking in code

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.240
and shifting responsibility
to others in the hierarchy,

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.580
like Cohen or CFO of
the Trump organization,

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.530
Allen Weisselberg, for instance.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.560
I mean we don't know what
is actually happening

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.280
inside the Trump
organization at this time.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.330
But that's why we should be wary

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:52.920
about drawing from conclusions about

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.429
whether the grand jury
will find evidence that

00:02:54.429 --> 00:02:56.879
Trump had an intent to commit crimes.

00:02:56.879 --> 00:02:58.570
But it is a possibility.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.830
So the question is,
"What is this grand jury

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.350
that's looking at Trump
actually doing right now?"

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.850
Well, first we have to start
with what is a grand jury?

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.240
A grand jury is a group of citizens

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.900
who must decide whether
there's enough evidence

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.664
for a person to be charged with a crime.

00:03:10.664 --> 00:03:12.700
It's a very different proceeding than

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.290
the jury trials you're
probably used to seeing on TV.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:16.820
So, let's start with the difference

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.360
between a petit jury and a grand jury.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.430
A Petit Jury is convened to decide

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:22.880
whether someone has committed a crime

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.919
or a defendant has civil liability.

00:03:24.919 --> 00:03:27.180
Petit juries listened to
the evidence presented

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.790
by both parties during a trial,

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.220
and then return a verdict.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:31.840
If a Petit jury finds the defendant guilty

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.050
then there'll be sentenced typically

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.350
with a fine imprisonment
or combination of both.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.460
A Petit jury is the jury
that you're probably

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.150
used to seeing on TV.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.001
In contrast, a grand jury does not

00:03:41.001 --> 00:03:43.151
determine someone's guilt or innocence.

00:03:43.151 --> 00:03:46.070
It also has no involvement in civil cases.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:47.981
This is a purely criminal issue.

00:03:47.981 --> 00:03:51.130
A grand jury hears evidence
presented by a prosecutor

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.140
that jury must determine

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.460
whether there is probable cause to believe

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.590
that the person or entity
has committed a crime.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.533
If the grand jury decides
that there is probable cause,

00:03:59.533 --> 00:04:02.130
then it will file a written
statement of a charge.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.470
This is called an "indictment".

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.470
If the grand jury finds
probable cause then,

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.462
the defendant will be
formally charged with a crime.

00:04:07.462 --> 00:04:10.800
And potentially there will
be an actual criminal trial

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.830
to determine their guilt or innocence.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:14.410
Now this isn't the way that it happens

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.913
in every case or in every state.

00:04:16.913 --> 00:04:19.128
Some states use what's
called an information

00:04:19.128 --> 00:04:21.151
which is basically just
an indictment that comes

00:04:21.151 --> 00:04:23.100
directly from the District Attorney

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.499
with no need for the
involvement of a grand jury.

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:28.340
But let's talk about New York law.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:30.220
In the New York constitution,

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:31.647
Article 1 Section 6 says that,

00:04:31.647 --> 00:04:34.740
"No defendant may be tried
for a felony without the case

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.387
first having been
presented to a grand jury."

00:04:37.387 --> 00:04:39.190
A New York grand jury is composed

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:41.890
of between 23 and 16 people.

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:43.590
These people are summoned
the same way that

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.608
anyone is for jury duty, with
a notice from a County Clerk.

00:04:46.608 --> 00:04:49.050
The jurors usually have to serve a term

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.090
that ranges from between two
weeks and several months.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.090
And a special grand jury can
meet for up to six months.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.560
And since this proceeding doesn't
determine someone's guilt,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:57.640
it only determines whether there's

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.289
probable cause for an indictment.

00:04:59.289 --> 00:05:00.838
You don't have a lot of the safeguards

00:05:00.838 --> 00:05:03.900
that you would normally have
in a full blown criminal trial.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.299
For example, under New York
law, there's no judge presiding

00:05:06.299 --> 00:05:08.249
in the room during the
grand jury proceedings.

00:05:08.249 --> 00:05:10.718
The prosecutor's in charge
and is therefore responsible

00:05:10.718 --> 00:05:12.345
for presenting the government's case

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.388
and reading the law to the grand jury.

00:05:14.388 --> 00:05:17.450
This means that Cy Vance's
office has two roles.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:18.636
First, presenting evidence

00:05:18.636 --> 00:05:20.630
as a District Attorney for Manhattan

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:23.030
and advising the grand jury about process.

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.709
The DA takes on the role of a judge

00:05:24.709 --> 00:05:26.910
by advising the jury and ruling on the

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.100
admissibility of evidence
and answering questions.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.180
Hopefully the DA will act in good faith

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:32.920
but if he believes that
there's probable cause,

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.880
he's going to present evidence
advocating for an indictment.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.117
This is why grand juries are so one-sided.

00:05:38.117 --> 00:05:41.455
So, who is in this
secretive grand jury room?

00:05:41.455 --> 00:05:43.518
Well, usually it's the jurors,

00:05:43.518 --> 00:05:45.424
the prosecutor, and a court reporter.

00:05:45.424 --> 00:05:47.708
Witnesses are only
present during questioning

00:05:47.708 --> 00:05:49.900
the defendant and his or her lawyers

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.360
are generally not present

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.800
but will get to an exception to that.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.330
And when the jury deliberates,

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.790
the prosecutors and court
reporter leave the room.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.270
Grand jurors can ask questions

00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:59.174
which is something that doesn't happen

00:05:59.174 --> 00:06:01.420
during criminal trial proceedings.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:04.070
However, the prosecutor
then puts on the judge hat

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:06.300
and decides whether the
question is relevant.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:07.880
And in a federal grand jury, yes,

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:10.426
the federal prosecutors are
bound by a similar procedure.

00:06:10.426 --> 00:06:11.848
The prosecutor is not constrained

00:06:11.848 --> 00:06:13.420
by the rules of evidence that applied

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:14.680
during the criminal trial.

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.130
But in New York, the prosecutor
is bound by the state's

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:19.350
rules of evidence with a
few noteworthy exceptions

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.728
which give more leeway
for hearsay testimony.

00:06:21.728 --> 00:06:24.460
If someone is indicted
based on hearsay testimony,

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.070
prosecutors will then have to develop

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.337
non-hearsay evidence
for the criminal case.

00:06:28.337 --> 00:06:31.050
Now, I know this sounds
incredibly one sided,

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.780
but remember, there's still a
criminal trial get to happen.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:35.610
This is only deciding about a

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.306
potential indictment and traditionally,

00:06:37.306 --> 00:06:39.799
a potential defendant
had no right to testify

00:06:39.799 --> 00:06:43.170
in his or her defense in
front of a grand jury.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:45.480
But, defendants in New
York do have a right to

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:46.780
testify before the grand jury.

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:49.210
But, this is almost never a good idea

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.830
because the bar is so
low to get an indictment.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.580
For example, New York
criminal procedure law

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.750
Section 190.50 provides
that a potential defendant

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:59.200
must be permitted to
testify before a grand jury.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.770
But, the potential defendant has to sign

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:04.670
and submit a waiver of
immunity and a waiver of his

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:07.490
or her fifth amendment right
against self-incrimination.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:08.620
And there's very little benefit

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.053
to testifying before a grand jury.

00:07:10.053 --> 00:07:11.871
A potential defendant
won't know the extent

00:07:11.871 --> 00:07:13.820
of the government's case against them.

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:15.322
And unlike a criminal trial process

00:07:15.322 --> 00:07:18.790
where the evidence has established
well in advance of trial.

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:20.780
And the prosecutor has
an obligation to give

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:22.100
that evidence to the defendants.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:23.803
Here, the person's testimony could tip off

00:07:23.803 --> 00:07:26.569
the prosecutor about additional
evidence and witnesses.

00:07:26.569 --> 00:07:28.870
And potentially worst
of all, the defendant's

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:32.180
testimony could also be used
in a future criminal trial.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:35.130
Meaning that the evidence
the person blindly offers

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:36.301
during a grand jury proceeding

00:07:36.301 --> 00:07:40.070
could be highly damning and
used against them in the future.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:41.697
Believe me, former
President Trump does not

00:07:41.697 --> 00:07:44.025
want to testify before any grand jury.

00:07:44.025 --> 00:07:46.130
Even though, as I'm sure he would say

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:49.270
the testimony would be
beautiful, perfect testimony.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.420
And perhaps most importantly
at the conclusion

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:52.665
of the proceedings, jurors will vote

00:07:52.665 --> 00:07:54.128
on whether there is probable cause

00:07:54.128 --> 00:07:56.463
that the potential
defendant committed a crime.

00:07:56.463 --> 00:07:59.580
They vote on whether to return
what is called a "true bill"

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.010
authorizing prosecutors
to indict the defendant.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:02.920
And in the grand jury,

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:05.450
prosecutors don't need
a unanimous grand jury

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:07.360
and affirmative vote
of just 12 grand jurors

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:08.756
is enough to authorize an indictment.

00:08:08.756 --> 00:08:10.835
And if 12 jurors don't believe

00:08:10.835 --> 00:08:12.710
enough evidence exists to charge a person,

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:14.750
then they can return
what's called a "no bill"

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:18.610
which prevents indictment
but, that's pretty unlikely.

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:20.730
But either way, everything
related to the proceeding

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:22.610
is supposed to just to
be sealed and secret,

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:24.088
which is interesting given that there were

00:08:24.088 --> 00:08:26.180
leaks with it led to the Washington Post

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:27.830
reporting that the grand jury exists

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:29.430
and is in process.

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:30.630
But let's talk about secrecy

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:31.887
in these grand jury proceedings.

00:08:31.887 --> 00:08:33.817
Both state and federal grand juries

00:08:33.817 --> 00:08:35.740
are conducted in secrecy.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:36.770
The Supreme COurt has laid out

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:38.510
many reasons for secret proceedings.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:40.786
Number 1, prevents the
escape potential defendants.

00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:43.100
It ensures that the
jurors are not pressured

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.140
by their friends or
threatened with retaliation

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:46.109
by potential defendants.

00:08:46.109 --> 00:08:47.789
It discourages perjury and encourages

00:08:47.789 --> 00:08:49.209
people to testify without fear.

00:08:49.209 --> 00:08:50.819
And it protects potential defendants

00:08:50.819 --> 00:08:53.406
who are not yet indicted
from the disclosure

00:08:53.406 --> 00:08:56.115
that they were investigated for a crime.

00:08:56.115 --> 00:08:57.410
Under New York law,

00:08:57.410 --> 00:08:59.669
the prosecutor and jurors are
not allowed to reveal anything

00:08:59.669 --> 00:09:03.270
about the presentation,
including testimony or voting.

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:06.030
It's a felony to disclose
grand jury information.

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.432
However, the secrecy
doesn't extend to witnesses.

00:09:08.432 --> 00:09:11.287
So, the hundred thousand dollars question,

00:09:11.287 --> 00:09:13.082
"Is an indictment against members

00:09:13.082 --> 00:09:14.508
of the Trump family imminent?"

00:09:14.508 --> 00:09:16.890
Well, a few words of caution about reports

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:18.620
that Donald Trump is about to be indicted.

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:20.741
First of all, Donald
Trump is potentially not

00:09:20.741 --> 00:09:22.850
the only target of this probe.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:25.200
He may not even be a target of this probe.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:26.850
Officers of the company, Don Jr.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:29.068
And CFO Allen Weisselberg
could be implicated

00:09:29.068 --> 00:09:32.240
as could Ivanka or Trump sisters.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:35.070
And of course, in a similarly
analogous situation,

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:37.050
people over-invested in
the Mueller investigation

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:39.100
only to be let down
when Mueller interpreted

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:41.218
his mandate very, very narrowly.

00:09:41.218 --> 00:09:43.467
Saying that he wasn't
allowed to say whether

00:09:43.467 --> 00:09:45.860
Trump committed a crime or not

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:49.160
which is a source of great
controversy to this day.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:51.950
And Cy Vance is not exactly
Captain America either.

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:54.800
In January 2020, advocates
called on Vance to

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.180
resign because of his pattern of allowing

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:58.664
wealthy and powerful
defendants off the hook.

00:09:58.664 --> 00:10:01.940
Vance declined to prosecute
Harvey Weinstein in 2015.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:04.080
He also allegedly bungled a case

